			<div class="content" id="post-content-1576549">
									

<p>Interesting. A few little things:</p>
<ul>
<li>The picture should really be labeled in accordance with the table: SCP-274-1-4</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>I don't think you mean &quot;sanitize&quot;. Technically, the SCP-274 being applied to the victim coats them, rather than removing microorganisms and dirt from their surface. You might get more mileage from the word &quot;encapsulate&quot;. Or, since it's being sprayed into their mouths, you could assume an anesthetic effect that would make them easier to consume &#8212; much the way cytotoxic T cells begin killing the bacteria or virus before the NK cell moves in to consume the target they've &quot;tagged&quot;.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Compulsions, even ones contracted by touch, are something of a sore point; if your creature or object has to have a compulsion in order to work, you might need to rewrite it. That's not to say there can never be a good SCP with a compulsion attached, but it works against you. Could the contaminated building pull in prey in another manner?</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>If SCP-274-2 don't have internal organs, they don't have a chest cavity; the &quot;torso&quot; area would be their center of mass, instead.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>You may wish to make another proofreading pass. (I'd handle it myself, but I'm babysitting and using a weak connection right now.) You typed &quot;contaminates&quot; for &quot;contaminants&quot;, for example.</li>
</ul>
<p>As pertains to the reasons you cited:</p>
<p>1. The Foundation is less &quot;above&quot; and more &quot;outside&quot; the law. If you're a secret extra-governmental agency, you try to avoid people whose entire job description centers around finding out what's going on.</p>
<p>2. Noted, though as I said, the compulsion is working against you here.</p>
<p>3. Not all biological structures are organs. In most complex organisms, you pass through at least one preparatory structure (like the mouth and teeth in humans) before entering the actual digestive tract. And technically, if there's no waste and no need to chew, you don't need a digestive tract&#8230; the cell simply engulfs the target organism and digests it.</p>
<p>4. Technically, they're not antibodies; they're viruses. They work to infect surrounding organisms and convert them into additional instances, making them viral. Or, if you take a different tack, they're worker ants bringing food back to the colony.</p>

							</div>

								
					
